an update now on steve duncagger the  crusading lawyer whose efforts to help  indigenous people in the amazon  rainforest in ecuador ended up getting  him disbarred and sent to prison for  contempt of court rhonda  brian donziger had refused to turn his  computer over to lawyers for chevron who  had accused him of dishonesty in gaining  a 10 billion dollar verdict in the  courts in ecuador he was sentenced to  six months in prison earlier this year  but in a surprise development he's now  been released and he's back home brian  and we're joined now by stephen dunsiger  no longer behind bars stephen how did  you get released  i i don't know exactly it's a bit of a  mystery i think the legal authority was  coveted um congress allows the bureau of  prisons to release  people who they think are at risk of  contracting covet  um early after serving 25 of their  sentence and when i say release brian i  don't mean released i'm not free they  release you to home confinement where  you're still under the custody of the  bureau of prisons as i am so i'm not  free i'm just serving out the rest of my  sentence at home  um the prison i was at in danbury was  overcrowded i mean there were a lot of  people there and i think that the prison  officials looking at the fact that i was  literally the only person in the entire  facility  there for a misdemeanor everybody else  was there for the felony they're like  you know my sentence was so short  they probably concluded like why are we  wasting a bet on someone who really  probably shouldn't be here to begin with  even before you went behind bars you had  served almost 800 days on house arrest  you still have another five months or so  i guess does it change your mind at all  about your stand and refusing to turn  over your devices your background  material electronic devices uh to the  lawyers for chevron  well that's a great question um the  short answer is no  but i have done a lot of reflection  about  how this all went down and whether you  know i might have made errors in  judgment on my end  um i'll say this i took a principled  stand to protect my clients i don't  believe i did anything wrong i believe  i'm innocent i still have a pending  appeal that i hope to win um  that said uh you know going forward if  this  issue continues i'm going to be  represented by council  and we are going to work our way through  it i have never  taken the position that i would never  turn my computer over to chevron in the  court order that was not the issue the  issue was whether upon turning it over  to chevron privileged information in my  computer would be protected that was the  issue  and you know that's still the issue and  it has to be sort of worked through but  you know obviously i  you know i believe i'm a man of the rule  of law i comply with court orders um you  know as you look back on things that go  down brian you know there's a lot of  things in life i mean if you obviously  if you knew certain things  that you know later you might have done  stuff differently but you know i stand  by my position i think it's the right  position the ethical position i'll  remind people no lawyer ever has been  charged with criminal contempt to court  for what i did which was essentially  taken appeal of a civil discovery order  chevron is not letting up though they  still want your devices and they still  have been pushing for you to be behind  bars i guess  well listen this whole idea originated  with chevron i mean that this was a  prosecution by a private law firm that  had chevron as a client after the  government refused to prosecute and yeah  you can look at it like hey why didn't  you turn your computer over you know and  that's one way to look at it another way  is like why are you being prosecuted by  a chevron law firm after you won a large  civil judgment on behalf of indigenous  peoples in the amazon against chevron  and i think this latter issue is really  what's important for all americans and  really all people in the world  and of course we should point out that  the prosecutors in the federal  prosecutors in the southern district of  new york declined to prosecute you but  the judge went ahead and appointed this  private lawyer to to do just that so and  let me ask you as you sat behind bars up  in danbury uh what were you thinking  well look you know on first of all on a  personal level it's challenging to be in  prison  um  you know it was far more difficult than  i expected frankly i mean it's a low  security prison but because of covert it  was pretty much locked down the whole  time i was there which means we couldn't  we couldn't get out side  also i'll say this the medical care is  really complicated particularly the age  of coburn i was worried i'm 60 years old  that if i got sick  you know it would be  not a good place not a good thing not  the right place for me and i really  wanted to get out of there and  as soon as i could and i'm thankful and  grateful to the bureau of prisons that  they made the decision that they did  which i think was based on policy and  was appropriate  the point of contempt of court and  sending you to prison obviously was to  try to break you to get you to comply  with the court order are you broken now  first of all i'm definitely not broken i  mean i'm um we're going to continue to  fight on there's so many lawyers from  around the world who have called me to  join our team where i think we're as  strong as ever  um partly as a result of what i would  call this overreach you know putting an  american human rights lawyer in prison  in terms of holding chevron accountable  for its pollution in the amazon which is  what drove me to work on this issue  you know there's still work to do we're  not broken to the contrary we're going  to keep moving forward until they pay  the judgment and the indigenous peoples  in the amazon can have their ancestral  lands restored and they can you know get  what they deserve which is compensation  for the harm caused them as ordered by  various courts around the world  and let me have you respond your chevron  continues to maintain allegedly you  bribed a judge you presented fabricated  evidence they didn't like your tactics  at all and in fact the judgment you  received in ecuador i guess has been  upended in this country  well look this these are the facts i'm  sure i disagree with chevron's view  obviously we've been at loggerheads for  years um you know we meaning me and  other group other lawyers on behalf of  these indigenous peoples won a 10  billion dollar pollution judgment after  an eight year trial that has been  affirmed by ecuador supreme court it's  been affirmed for enforcement purposes  by canada's supreme court and all 28  appellate judges there's one judge in  the u.s that they always cite judge  kaplan who ruled against me after a  non-jury trial where they paid a witness  to claim i bribed a judge it didn't  happen it's been rejected by 28  appellate judges in other countries it  did not happen definitely did did not  happen  no it didn't there was no bribery of a  judge  they paid a witness to say that about me  but it did not happen there's no  corroborating evidence  judges serious judges all over the world  have looked at this and rejected it  so you know it's just not true  now it is a judicial ruling here by this  one judge and they try to play on it  constantly when they give statements to  journalists but it's simply not true the  judgment is legitimate it's been  affirmed by six different appellate  courts and it's owned by the it's not my  judgment it's the judgment of the people  of ecuador they have lawyers and their  intention as i understand it is to make  sure that judgment is enforced so the  money is paid and chevron is forced to  comply with the rule of law  and finally just to uh how are you doing  now out of prison back with your family  for the holidays how are you doing  personally i'm i'm  thrilled  to be home  um i i can't explain it i i i've been  swirling with emotion you know since i  got out  um i've been  i felt i i realized how much pain i  carried out of that prison i i mean the  experience  to see so many people hurt inside and to  come out see my family was hurting  and i'll say this you know going into  prison is often is harder on the people  who love you on the outside than it is  on you on the inside i've realized that  a lot of people told me that it's really  true  you know i could at least control to an  extent what i did inside you know but  when you're outside you're like oh my  god what's going on is he you know  there's no communication you know is he  even sleeping and i can tell you i  didn't have one decent night's sleep in  45 days it was it was  really unsettling  um i do want to acknowledge though that  it's you know i met a lot of strong  people in prison who  you know i learned from them how they  handled the time and my sentence  obviously was ridiculously short  compared to most people  um but to me it felt like an eternity  i'm really happy to be home and i'm  happy to be with my family and i'm happy  to be able to  speak again to people like you so thank  you for asking  all right stephen danzano thank you so  much for being with us again be here  tonight  thank you  up next inside the proud boys and the  oath keepers in america's other violent  extremist groups will be joined by the  photojournalist who has been tracking  them you're watching brian ross  investigates on the long crime trial  network  hi this is dan abrams with exciting news  for all of our law and crime followers  on youtube you can now get the live law  and crime network with youtube tv for  all of your daily live trial coverage  legal news expert analysis and original  true crime programs subscribe to youtube  tv today and then locate law and crime  in the channel guide and for only 1.99 a  month you can add the network to your  bundle watch lawn crime ever